//   - Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, table of contents:
//       https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/TOC.html
//   - Telgarsky, Deep Learning Theory lecture notes (UIUC), contents:
//       https://mjt.cs.illinois.edu/dlt/
//   - Princeton COS 597B Theoretical Deep Learning (Arora, Fall 2019), lecture list:
//       https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall19/cos597B
//   - Cambridge Part II Mathematics of Machine Learning schedule:
//       https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/undergrad/course/schedules.pdf
//   - Wikipedia, Universal approximation theorem (width/depth variants and attributions):
//       https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_approximation_theorem
// Gaps filled after comparing: finite-sample memorization, deep-narrow universality, Yarotsky’s x²
// construction, VC dimension of ReLU networks and norm-based margin bounds (Telgarsky),
// scale invariance under normalization and gradient descent–ascent cycling (Arora), implicit
// differentiation, data augmentation and ensembles (both references).
// Deliberate omissions: reinforcement learning (Prince ch. 19; belongs with `ml`/`ctrl`); ethics
// (Prince ch. 21); Boltzmann machines and other energy-based models (Goodfellow ch. 16–20, mostly
// historical); the Kolmogorov–Arnold representation theorem (no honest constructive picture);
// generic VC/Rademacher theory (lives in `ml`); convex-optimization rates (live in `opt`).
